WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.652
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:05.952 --> 00:00:09.280
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:16.552
Unit nineteen: Freedom of the Press. Today you will link past causes to present results with mixed conditionals.

4
00:00:16.852 --> 00:00:19.469
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.817
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:25.975
Press censorship.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:31.189
Press censorship means government control over what newspapers may publish.

8
00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.259
Press censorship hid the scandal for years.

9
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.978
Your turn. Say: Press censorship.

10
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:42.136
Press censorship.

11
00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:44.535
Investigative freedom.

12
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:50.591
Investigative freedom means the freedom of reporters to research powerful people.

13
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:53.787
Investigative freedom exposed the fraud.

14
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:57.279
Your turn. Say: Investigative freedom.

15
00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:01.970
Investigative freedom.

16
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:04.449
Government transparency.

17
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.087
Government transparency means openness about what the government does.

18
00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:13.517
Government transparency lets journalists check official claims.

19
00:01:14.117 --> 00:01:17.675
Your turn. Say: Government transparency.

20
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:22.463
Government transparency.

21
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:24.485
Journalist safety.

22
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:29.340
Journalist safety means protection of reporters from harm and threats.

23
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.006
Journalist safety is a growing concern worldwide.

24
00:01:33.617 --> 00:01:36.722
Your turn. Say: Journalist safety.

25
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:40.928
Journalist safety.

26
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:43.323
Media independence.

27
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:48.592
Media independence means freedom of news organisations from outside control.

28
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:51.752
Media independence depends on fair funding.

29
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.258
Your turn. Say: Media independence.

30
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.693
Media independence.

31
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.799
Public's right to know.

32
00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:07.639
Public's right to know means the idea that citizens deserve access to important information.

33
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.503
The public's right to know justified publishing the report.

34
00:02:12.117 --> 00:02:15.106
Your turn. Say: Public's right to know.

35
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:19.362
Public's right to know.

36
00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:21.466
Press regulation.

37
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.294
Press regulation means rules that govern how newspapers and broadcasters behave.

38
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:31.415
Press regulation should punish lies, not criticism.

39
00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:34.982
Your turn. Say: Press regulation.

40
00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:39.136
Press regulation.

41
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:41.093
Free expression.

42
00:02:42.083 --> 00:02:45.766
Free expression means the right to share ideas and opinions openly.

43
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.930
Free expression is essential in a democracy.

44
00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:52.308
Your turn. Say: Free expression.

45
00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:56.365
Free expression.

46
00:02:57.167 --> 00:03:00.852
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.717 --> 00:03:09.326
Visal, an editor says 'If the government had not censored the story, the public would be better informed today.' Which conditional is that?

48
00:03:10.417 --> 00:03:21.083
It is a mixed conditional. The if-clause refers to the past (had not censored), but the result refers to the present (would be informed today). The two times are different.

49
00:03:22.183 --> 00:03:23.927
Is there another mixed type?

50
00:03:25.017 --> 00:03:37.677
Yes, present condition and past result: If journalists were not afraid, they would have published the report last year. Here a permanent situation explains a past event.

51
00:03:38.783 --> 00:03:40.017
How do I choose?

52
00:03:41.117 --> 00:03:57.149
Ask when each part happens. Past cause, present result: had + would. Present state, past result: past simple + would have. It makes arguments about press regulation and media independence sharper.

53
00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.696
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:05.081
Grammar focus: Mixed conditionals: past cause, present result.

55
00:04:05.381 --> 00:04:06.602
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:07.317 --> 00:04:11.569
If the government had not censored the story, the public would be better informed today.

57
00:04:12.483 --> 00:04:16.491
If journalists were not afraid, they would have published the report last year.

58
00:04:17.383 --> 00:04:21.182
If the law had protected reporters, press freedom would be stronger now.

59
00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:33.212
Mixed conditional: if + past perfect (past cause) + would + base (present result); or if + past simple (present state) + would have + past participle (past result).

60
00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:38.425
Be careful! Don't say: If he studied law, he would be judge now.

61
00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:43.434
Say: If he had studied law, he would be a judge now.

62
00:04:43.734 --> 00:04:47.255
Past cause: use had + past participle.

63
00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:50.870
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.655
Why Journalists Need Protection.

65
00:04:53.955 --> 00:04:56.871
A free press is the public's right to know in action.

66
00:04:57.867 --> 00:05:03.036
If press censorship had never been introduced, many scandals would be common knowledge now.

67
00:05:04.033 --> 00:05:09.423
If journalist safety were guaranteed, more reporters would have investigated powerful people years ago.

68
00:05:10.433 --> 00:05:17.971
Government transparency and media independence allow investigative freedom to work, while sensible press regulation prevents abuse.

69
00:05:18.967 --> 00:05:22.684
Free expression, once lost, is very hard to restore.

70
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.674
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:28.517 --> 00:05:31.782
If the story had not been censored, we blank informed today.

72
00:05:32.082 --> 00:05:35.329
A, will be. B, would have been. C, would be.

73
00:05:38.867 --> 00:05:40.924
The answer is C: would be.

74
00:05:42.083 --> 00:05:45.453
If reporters were safe, they blank the story last year.

75
00:05:45.753 --> 00:05:50.242
A, would publish. B, would have published. C, had published.

76
00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.136
The answer is B: would have published.

77
00:05:57.283 --> 00:06:00.432
Government control over what may be published is press blank.

78
00:06:00.732 --> 00:06:05.094
A, censorship. B, regulation. C, transparency.

79
00:06:08.633 --> 00:06:10.610
The answer is A: censorship.

80
00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:13.750
Choose the correct mixed conditional.

81
00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:23.787
A, If he studied law, he would be a judge now.. B, If he had studied law, he would be a judge now.. C, If he had studied law, he will be a judge now..

82
00:06:27.333 --> 00:06:31.469
The answer is B: If he had studied law, he would be a judge now..

83
00:06:32.617 --> 00:06:36.671
According to the reading, free expression is hard to blank once lost.

84
00:06:36.971 --> 00:06:40.659
A, ignore. B, measure. C, restore.

85
00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.571
The answer is C: restore.

86
00:06:47.767 --> 00:06:50.918
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:51.783 --> 00:06:56.186
Why is journalist safety considered essential to a healthy, functioning democracy?

88
00:07:04.983 --> 00:07:06.681
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:06.981 --> 00:07:32.789
Journalists inform citizens about what powerful people do. If reporters fear violence, arrest or lawsuits, they may avoid important stories, and corruption can stay hidden. Safe journalists can investigate honestly, which helps voters make informed choices and officials stay accountable. Protecting them is therefore protecting the public's right to know.

90
00:07:33.089 --> 00:07:34.740
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:41.600
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and mixed conditionals: past cause, present result.

92
00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:46.375
Today's value is moral courage. Do what is right even when it is difficult.

93
00:07:46.675 --> 00:07:49.269
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:52.802
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

